<h2>The statutory route, step by step</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>1. Application filed, date fixed</h3><p>On a Section 12 application, the Magistrate fixes the first date of hearing — Section 12(4) says ordinarily within three days of the court receiving the application.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>2. Notice goes to the Protection Officer</h3><p>Section 13(1): the Magistrate gives the notice of the hearing date to the Protection Officer.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>3. Service within two days</h3><p>The Protection Officer gets the notice served on the respondent — and on any other person the court directs — by the prescribed means, within a maximum of two days or the further reasonable time the Magistrate allows.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>4. Declaration of service</h3><p>Section 13(2): the Protection Officer\'s declaration of service is proof of service, unless the contrary is shown.</p></div>
