<h2>Why the Act built its own machinery</h2>
<p>The DV Act was designed as a fast civil-protective remedy administered through criminal courts, and ordinary summons practice was one of the delays it set out to bypass. Routing service through the Protection Officer — an officer under the court\'s direction with statutory duties to the aggrieved person — keeps the first hearing meaningful and makes early interim relief under Section 23 practically possible. The prescribed means of service under the Rules framed in 2006 contemplate service through the Protection Officer and other channels, and in working practice in Delhi, courts frequently take the assistance of the SHO of the police station where the respondent resides or works, so that the two-day expectation has real teeth.</p>

<h2>Where service battles are actually fought</h2>
<p>Three recurring situations generate most of the litigation around Section 13. The first is the <strong>disputed address</strong>: the respondent has moved, works elsewhere, or the household has fractured across multiple premises. The answer is candour and breadth — give the court every known residential and work address, and ask that service be effected at each. The second is <strong>evasion</strong>: repeated reports that the respondent is &ldquo;not found&rdquo; at an address where he plainly lives. Courts respond by recording the attempts and moving to substituted modes consistent with the procedure made applicable by Section 28 — affixation, and in appropriate cases service through electronic means where the court is satisfied the channel reaches the respondent. The third is the <strong>respondent abroad</strong>, where service takes longer and courts calibrate the timeline, but the proceedings do not become impossible merely because the respondent is outside India.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>For respondents:</strong> ignoring a DV notice is the most expensive mistake available. Under Section 13(2), the Protection Officer\'s declaration stands as proof of service unless displaced, and a respondent who sits out the first hearings invites ex parte interim orders — maintenance, residence directions, protection orders — that operate immediately and must then be challenged or set aside on terms. The far better course on receiving a notice, by whatever channel, is to appear, take copies of the application and the Domestic Incident Report, and contest on the merits from day one.</p>
</div>

<h2>Checklist at the first hearing</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>For the aggrieved person:</strong> verify the service report is on record; if service failed, supply alternative addresses immediately and seek police assistance for the next attempt.</li>
  <li><strong>Ask for interim relief without waiting:</strong> Section 23 permits ex parte interim orders on the applicant\'s affidavit where the case warrants, so defective service of the respondent need not leave the applicant unprotected in the interim.</li>
  <li><strong>For the respondent:</strong> inspect the declaration of service before disputing it — a bare denial will not displace Section 13(2); be ready to show where you actually resided on the relevant dates.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep addresses current:</strong> both sides should place on record any change of address during the proceedings; stale addresses are the single biggest cause of avoidable delay in DV matters.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>The larger point</h2>
<p>Service is not a formality in DV litigation; it is the hinge on which the Act\'s sixty-day aspiration under Section 12(5) turns. A well-prepared applicant treats Section 13 as part of case strategy — complete addresses, early police assistance, prompt resort to substituted service — while a well-advised respondent treats the first notice as the start of the defence, not an inconvenience to be dodged. Either way, the Protection Officer\'s declaration under Section 13(2) is the document around which later disputes will revolve, and both sides should know exactly what it says.</p>
